{% extends "layouts/docs" %}

{% block content %}
	<p><code>flush</code> closes the <a href="/docs/tags/outlet/">outlets</a> above it. When you <a href="/docs/api/streaming/">stream</a> a page, everything up to <code>flush</code> can then be sent, instead of waiting for the render to end.</p>

	{% apply example %}
		{% verbatim %}
<html>
	<head>
		{% outlet "head" %}
		{% add to "head" %}
			<title>Home</title>
		{% endadd %}
	</head>
	{% flush %}
	<body>
		<h1>Home</h1>
	</body>
</html>
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p><code>flush</code> outputs nothing. It closes outlets with <code>render</code> too, so a template gives the same HTML and the same errors whether you render or stream it.</p>

	<h2>Closed outlets</h2>

	<ul>
		<li>Each outlet above <code>flush</code> gets the content added to it so far, and is then closed.</li>
		<li>Adding content to a closed outlet is an <code>outlet-closed</code> error, because that part of the page may already have been sent.</li>
		<li>An <code>add to … once</code> tag that already added its content is fine, because it adds nothing new.</li>
		<li>Outlets below <code>flush</code> stay open.</li>
	</ul>

	<p>Put <code>flush</code> after the last tag that adds to the outlets above it. Components lower in the page can add to an outlet that comes after it instead, such as one before <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code>.</p>

	<h2>Rules</h2>

	<ul>
		<li><code>flush</code> can be used any number of times.</li>
		<li>It can't be inside <code>cache</code>, <code>set</code>, <code>apply</code>, <code>add to</code> or a macro, because their content isn't part of the page yet.</li>
	</ul>
{% endblock %}
